# Modules across files: `import`, and the names it brings into scope.
#
#   ./bin/crystal run samples/iyi/modules.iyi
#
# Everything below compiles and runs today. `hello.iyi` covers traits and
# `impl` in a single file; this one covers the part a single file cannot show.

module samples/modules

# R-1: `import` names a module and loads it at most once, so the import graph
# is a DAG rather than textual inclusion. Bare, it brings the module into
# scope under its qualified name and nothing more: after `import app/greeter`
# alone, the module's names are reachable only as `App::Greeter::Greet` and
# `App::Greeter.polite`.
import app/greeter::*
import app/formal::{address}

# II.3: `::*` and `::{...}` are what make those names available unqualified.
# They are written by the consumer, not performed by the library: a bare
# import never changes what the importer's unqualified names mean, so a
# library cannot capture a name in your file by adding one to itself.

# The selective form narrows an import's names to the ones listed. Here it
# is not decoration — `app/greeter` and `app/formal` both export `title`, and
# taking both whole would make every unqualified `title` in this module
# ambiguous.

pub struct User
  getter name : String

  def initialize(@name : String)
  end
end

# `Greet` resolves through `import app/greeter::*` above, so `impl` needs no
# qualification.
#
# R-3's orphan rule allows this impl here because `User` is declared here:
# this is the type's module. It would equally be allowed in `app/greeter`,
# the trait's module. Nowhere else — a third module that imports both is
# refused, which is what makes two modules unable to define the same impl.
impl Greet for User
  def greet : String
    # `polite` comes from `app/greeter`, `address` from `app/formal`, and
    # `shout` is this module's own function, declared below. All three are
    # written the same way, because to this file they are all just names.
    shout(polite(name)) + " " + address(name)
  end
end

# A module's own functions are in scope for the types declared in it, without
# `self.` and without qualification: an iyi module is a compilation unit, and
# these are its functions. Not exported, so nothing outside can call it.
def shout(text : String) : String
  text.upcase
end

puts User.new("iyi").greet

# `title` was reachable unqualified from neither import: `app/greeter`'s was
# left ambiguous by nothing (only one import was whole), and `app/formal`'s
# was excluded by the selective form. Qualification always works.
puts App::Greeter.title
puts App::Formal.title

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this sample would report as an error
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * Widening the second import to `import app/formal::*` and then writing an
#   unqualified `title` is an error, and it is reported at the `title`, not at
#   the import. Widening it alone is not an error at all: two modules
#   exporting the same name is not by itself a mistake, so nothing is wrong
#   until a name is written that could mean either (SPEC.md II.3).
#
# * Adding `def title` here makes even that legal again. A local definition
#   beats an imported one, silently, which is what keeps a library from
#   breaking its consumers by adding an export.
#
# * Moving the `impl` above into a module that is neither `app/greeter` nor
#   this one is refused by R-3, naming both modules where it would be allowed.
#
# * `import app/greeter::{greeting}` — `greeting` is declared there without
#   `pub`, so it is not part of the module's surface. The error names it at the
#   import, where the author can see which of the names they asked for they
#   cannot have. Widening to `import app/greeter::*` does not reach it
#   either; it simply is not there to be found (R-2b).
#
# * `App::Greeter.greeting` — qualifying it does not get around `pub` either.
#   It has to be unreachable, not merely absent from an import's names:
#   `.iyimod` carries a module's exports and nothing else, so if another
#   module could call an unmarked name, that metadata would not be enough to
#   compile against (SPEC.md IV.2).
#
# * `import app/v_1`, or any path segment that is not `[a-z][a-z0-9]*` with
#   single `_` between groups. A module declared `app/greeter` is reached as
#   `App::Greeter`, and the two notations are kept in step by making the
#   mapping reversible: the name splits back into a path at every upper-case
#   letter. `camelcase` drops an underscore before a digit, so `v_1` and `v1`
#   would both be `V1` — two paths naming one module (SPEC.md IV.6 #6).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiling this file without `app/greeter.iyi`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# R-1 says a module that imports another reads its `.iyimod` and never opens
# its source. That is not a claim about a future release:
#
#     ./bin/crystal build --no-codegen --emit-iyimod /tmp/mods samples/iyi/modules.iyi
#     ./bin/crystal mod dump --declarations /tmp/mods/app/greeter.iyimod
#     mv samples/iyi/app /tmp/app-elsewhere
#     ./bin/crystal build --use-iyimod /tmp/mods samples/iyi/modules.iyi   # compiles
#     mv /tmp/app-elsewhere samples/iyi/app
#
# The second command prints what the third one compiles against: `app/greeter`
# with `pub def polite(name : String) : String`, `pub trait Greet` with its
# requirement, and no bodies at all. `greeting` is not in it — it carries no
# `pub`, and R-2b's "unreachable rather than absent from an import's names"
# is the same statement as "not in the artifact".
#
# * Not yet: object code. `--use-iyimod` implies `--no-codegen`, because the
#   artifact carries what another module may call and not how it is done, so
#   there is nothing in it to generate a program from (SPEC.md IV.1f).
